) VALUES (
  'English Grammar & Error Correction Challenge',
  'A challenge containing 20 curated English Grammar & Error Correction questions testing subject-verb agreement, modifiers, correlative conjunctions, pronouns, prepositions, parallel structure, and conditional statements.',
  '82d33ae3-f8f3-4ce6-bcf9-49acb05e9c55'::uuid,
  false,
  'all',
  'bed777fc-ed74-45b8-be38-1fd87e5d222d'::uuid,
  'easy',
  '["Full Name"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"question_text":"Being the best tennis player (A) / he was invited (B) / by the local sports club (C) / to play a friendly match on sunday. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The word \"Sunday\" is a proper noun and must always begin with a capital letter (''Sunday'' instead of ''sunday'')."]},{"question_text":"The principal along with the teachers (A) / were seen boarding a bus (B) / go to picnic (C) / on a national holiday. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["","When two subjects are connected by phrases like along with, with, together with, or as well as, the verb always agrees with the first subject. Since \"The principal\" is singular, the plural verb \"were\" must be replaced with the singular verb \"was\". (Note: Part C also contains an idiom error, \"go to picnic\" should be \"to go on a picnic\", but subject-verb agreement in B is the primary structural error).","",""]},{"question_text":"Even after hearing the leader (A) / for a long time (B) / the followers could not make out (C) / which he was talking about. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The relative pronoun \"which\" is used for selection among choices. Here, the sentence refers to the general subject matter of the speech, so the relative pronoun \"what\" should be used instead of \"which\"."]},{"question_text":"Contrary to earlier reports (A) / I found him (B) / brighter than I (C) / during our meeting. / No Error. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The sentence is grammatically correct. In formal English, when a comparison is made using \"than\", it can be followed by a subject pronoun (\"than I\") with the verb implied (brighter than I [am])."]},{"question_text":"With the introduction of the new syllabus (A) / the number of colleges reporting (B) / high results is decreasing (C) / year after year. / No Error. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The sentence is error-free. The phrase \"the number of\" always takes a singular verb, so \"is decreasing\" in part C is absolutely correct."]},{"question_text":"No sooner he left the office (A) / than it started (B) / raining heavily (C) / to make him completely wet. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Sentences beginning with the negative adverbial phrase \"No sooner\" must follow the rule of inversion, where the auxiliary verb comes before the subject. It should be written as \"No sooner had he left...\" or \"No sooner did he leave...\".","","",""]},{"question_text":"No boy (A) / in the class is (B) / as bright (C) / as his son. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["When comparing one person or thing with all others of the same group using \"as... as\" or \"so... as\", the word \"other\" must be included to exclude the person being compared from the rest of the group. Therefore, \"No boy\" should be replaced with \"No other boy\".","","",""]},{"question_text":"There is no doubt (A) / that the majority of workers (B) / is in favour (C) / of increasing the wages. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["","","The phrase \"the majority of\" followed by a plural countable noun (\"workers\") takes a plural verb. Therefore, \"is\" should be replaced with \"are\".",""]},{"question_text":"Just before the race (A) / I asked him (B) / whom he thought (C) / was going to win. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["","","\"He thought\" acts as a parenthetical clause here. The pronoun needed is the subject of the verb phrase \"was going to win\". Therefore, the objective pronoun \"whom\" should be replaced with the subjective pronoun \"who\".",""]},{"question_text":"How do you say (A) / that neither he or (B) / Ramesh has qualified (C) / in the examination. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["","The correlative conjunctions always go in pairs. The correct pair for \"neither\" is \"nor\". Therefore, \"or\" must be replaced with \"nor\".","",""]},{"question_text":"What this company needs (A) / is not only qualified (B) / man–power but also (C) / dedicated workers. / No Error. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The sentence is grammatically correct. The correlative conjunction pair \"not only... but also\" is used properly to connect two parallel noun phrases (\"qualified man-power\" and \"dedicated workers\")."]},{"question_text":"The captain and his (A) / wife were invited (B) /for the cultural (C) / function at my house. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["","","The verb \"invite\" is followed by the preposition \"to\" when referencing an event or destination. Therefore, \"invited for\" should be changed to \"invited to\".",""]},{"question_text":"I prefer (A) / interacting with (B) / computers to (C) / human being. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","To maintain grammatical parallelism in the comparison, the plural noun \"computers\" must be balanced with a plural noun on the other side. Therefore, \"human being\" should be changed to \"human beings\"."]},{"question_text":"Being a sunday (A) / he went out (B) / for a trip to Pune (C) / with his family. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["This sentence contains a dangling/misrelated participle. The implied subject of \"Being a Sunday\" becomes \"he\", which is incorrect because a person cannot be a Sunday. An independent pronoun is required to clear the weather/day reference, so it should be \"It being a Sunday\".","","",""]},{"question_text":"No one in (A) the locality (B) / is as intelligent (C) / a Ashok is. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The correct comparative construction is \"as + adjective + as\". Part D contains a typo/grammatical error where \"a\" is used instead of \"as\". It should be rewritten as \"as Ashok is\"."]},{"question_text":"If I was you (A) / I would tell (B) / the Chairman to keep (C) / his mouth shut. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["This is a second conditional sentence expressing an imaginary or hypothetical situation. In the subjunctive mood, the past plural form \"were\" is used for all subjects, including \"I\". Thus, \"If I was you\" should be \"If I were you\".","","",""]},{"question_text":"No sooner did (A) / he reached (B) / the station than (C) / the train departed. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["","The auxiliary verb \"did\" is always followed by the base form (first form) of the main verb. Therefore, the past tense \"reached\" must be replaced with the base form \"reach\".","",""]},{"question_text":"One of my friends (A) / is seriously ill and (B) /is in (C) / the intensive care unit. / No Error. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The sentence is completely correct. The phrase \"One of my\" is properly followed by a plural noun (\"friends\") and a singular verb \"is\"."]},{"question_text":"Are (A) / there any further (B) / reasons you can give (C) / us for your failure? / No Error. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["","","","The sentence is grammatically correct. \"Further\" is used correctly to mean \"additional\" or \"extra\" reasons."]},{"question_text":"If one of the candidates choose (A) / to leave before the examination is over (B) / allow him (C) / to do so. (D)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["The phrase \"one of the candidates\" refers to a single individual out of the group, making the subject singular. Therefore, it requires the singular verb \"chooses\" instead of the plural form \"choose\".","","",""]}]'::jsonb,
  '2026-07-14T10:49:00+05:30'::timestamptz,
  '2026-07-14T11:50:00+05:30'::timestamptz,
  60
);
